WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.894
You type a question. A moment later,

00:00:02.894 --> 00:00:04.041
an answer appears.

00:00:04.321 --> 00:00:07.784
It feels like the AI lives somewhere inside the screen.

00:00:07.784 --> 00:00:10.176
Pure software, made of nothing at all.

00:00:10.456 --> 00:00:12.477
But AI isn't just software.

00:00:12.977 --> 00:00:14.822
Thank you, Randy. Anyway.

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:16.894
Yes, AI lives in software.

00:00:16.894 --> 00:00:19.594
But software on its own can't do anything.

00:00:19.594 --> 00:00:22.037
It needs a physical machine to run on.

00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:26.699
Behind every answer are processors, memory, servers and networks.

00:00:26.979 --> 00:00:29.486
Here's the whole idea in four lines.

00:00:29.766 --> 00:00:32.838
Software provides the instructions and the models.

00:00:33.118 --> 00:00:36.673
Hardware provides the physical resources to execute them.

00:00:36.953 --> 00:00:39.501
Data moves through hardware components.

00:00:39.781 --> 00:00:44.366
And processing units perform the calculations that make AI possible.

00:00:44.646 --> 00:00:46.534
Let's take that machine apart,

00:00:46.534 --> 00:00:47.792
one piece at a time.

00:00:49.072 --> 00:00:51.261
Part two. The physical foundation.

00:00:51.861 --> 00:00:54.102
Open up any computer that runs AI,

00:00:54.102 --> 00:00:57.003
and you'll find the same cast of characters.

00:00:57.283 --> 00:00:59.969
The motherboard is the city they all live in.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:03.192
It connects every component and lets them communicate.

00:01:03.472 --> 00:01:05.555
The CPU is the coordinator.

00:01:05.555 --> 00:01:10.183
It manages operations and runs general-purpose instructions.

00:01:10.463 --> 00:01:12.304
The GPU is the specialist.

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:15.490
Thousands of small cores perform huge numbers

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.827
of calculations at the same time,

00:01:17.827 --> 00:01:18.676
in parallel.

00:01:18.956 --> 00:01:21.993
RAM temporarily holds the data and model

00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:24.802
information that is in use right now.

00:01:25.082 --> 00:01:28.450
The SSD stores AI models, datasets and software,

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.344
even when the power is off.

00:01:30.624 --> 00:01:33.462
The power supply provides the electricity.

00:01:33.742 --> 00:01:38.876
And the cooling system stops everything from overheating during intensive processing.

00:01:40.156 --> 00:01:42.556
Part three. How hardware runs AI.

00:01:43.156 --> 00:01:45.558
So what is all this hardware actually

00:01:45.558 --> 00:01:47.764
doing when an AI model is working?

00:01:47.764 --> 00:01:50.361
Mostly, maths. An enormous amount of it.

00:01:50.641 --> 00:01:54.153
At the heart of modern AI is matrix multiplication.

00:01:54.153 --> 00:01:57.941
Grids of numbers, multiplied by other grids of numbers,

00:01:57.941 --> 00:01:59.525
billions of times over.

00:01:59.805 --> 00:02:02.434
Let's follow one question through the machine.

00:02:02.714 --> 00:02:05.480
Step one. The data enters the computer.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.940
Step two. It's converted into numbers,

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.867
because numbers are all the hardware can work with.

00:02:11.147 --> 00:02:15.140
Step three. The processor calculates, using the model's parameters:

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.404
the values it learned during training.

00:02:17.684 --> 00:02:20.893
Step four. Intermediate results are stored in memory.

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:25.246
Step five. Further calculations are performed, again and again.

00:02:25.526 --> 00:02:28.574
Step six. The final output is generated.

00:02:28.854 --> 00:02:32.858
Those repeated calculations happen inside a neural network:

00:02:32.858 --> 00:02:36.590
interconnected computational units, arranged in layers.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.520
Each unit combines the numbers it receives,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.862
and passes a result to the next layer.

00:02:42.142 --> 00:02:43.822
The network is the recipe.

00:02:43.822 --> 00:02:47.635
The hardware is the kitchen that actually does the cooking,

00:02:47.635 --> 00:02:49.315
one calculation at a time.

00:02:50.595 --> 00:02:52.768
Part four. Where storage comes in.

00:02:53.368 --> 00:02:56.060
But processing power alone isn't enough.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.482
A processor has to be fed with data,

00:02:58.482 --> 00:03:00.030
quickly and constantly.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.607
Think of the processor as a worker.

00:03:02.887 --> 00:03:06.040
Storage is the warehouse, where everything is kept.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:07.785
RAM is the workbench,

00:03:07.785 --> 00:03:10.574
holding what the worker needs right now.

00:03:10.854 --> 00:03:13.449
And the faster information moves between them,

00:03:13.449 --> 00:03:15.931
the more efficiently the worker can operate.

00:03:16.211 --> 00:03:17.523
In a real machine,

00:03:17.523 --> 00:03:20.947
the SSD stores the trained models and datasets.

00:03:20.947 --> 00:03:23.715
RAM temporarily holds the active data.

00:03:23.995 --> 00:03:27.419
A GPU has its own fast memory, called VRAM,

00:03:27.419 --> 00:03:29.251
right beside its cores.

00:03:29.531 --> 00:03:32.571
And memory bandwidth is the road between them:

00:03:32.571 --> 00:03:34.950
how much data can move every second.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.267
Give the worker a tiny workbench,

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:39.243
or a slow road to the warehouse,

00:03:39.243 --> 00:03:41.651
and it spends most of its time waiting.

00:03:41.931 --> 00:03:44.585
That's why a powerful processor can still

00:03:44.585 --> 00:03:46.657
be limited by too little memory,

00:03:46.657 --> 00:03:48.081
or slow data transfer.

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:51.089
Part five. Networking.

00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:52.700
Here's a secret.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.721
The AI on your phone often isn't

00:03:54.721 --> 00:03:56.489
really on your phone at all.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:04:00.720
Many AI applications rely on more than one computer.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.423
When you send a request,

00:04:02.423 --> 00:04:04.438
your phone turns it into a signal.

00:04:04.718 --> 00:04:07.914
Routers and switches direct it across the network,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:11.238
often as pulses of light through fibre-optic cables.

00:04:11.518 --> 00:04:13.285
It arrives at a data centre:

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:15.242
a building packed with servers.

00:04:15.522 --> 00:04:18.794
A network interface card receives the request,

00:04:18.794 --> 00:04:20.288
and GPUs get to work.

00:04:20.568 --> 00:04:23.460
Then the answer makes the same journey back to you.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.820
Using remote hardware like this, over a network,

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.489
is called cloud computing.

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:32.348
Part six. Hardware in the physical world.

00:04:32.948 --> 00:04:37.144
AI hardware isn't only found in computers and data centres.

00:04:37.144 --> 00:04:39.988
Some AI has to deal with the real world.

00:04:40.268 --> 00:04:41.992
For that, it needs senses.

00:04:42.272 --> 00:04:46.383
Cameras capture visual information. Microphones capture sound.

00:04:46.663 --> 00:04:50.271
LiDAR and ultrasonic sensors measure distance.

00:04:50.271 --> 00:04:52.545
GPS modules provide location.

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:55.906
And temperature sensors monitor the environment.

00:04:56.186 --> 00:04:57.744
It also needs muscles.

00:04:57.744 --> 00:05:01.923
Motors and actuators let machines perform physical actions.

00:05:02.203 --> 00:05:05.915
Sending every reading to a distant server takes time.

00:05:05.915 --> 00:05:06.685
So instead,

00:05:06.685 --> 00:05:10.257
the AI model can run directly on the device itself.

00:05:10.537 --> 00:05:12.506
This is called edge AI.

00:05:12.786 --> 00:05:16.375
You'll find it in autonomous vehicles, smart cameras,

00:05:16.375 --> 00:05:19.762
industrial robots, drones, and smart home devices.

00:05:20.042 --> 00:05:23.555
Picture a small rover. Its sensors read the terrain.

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:26.392
Its onboard processor decides where to go.

00:05:26.672 --> 00:05:29.077
Its network link reports back to base.

00:05:29.077 --> 00:05:31.039
And its motors turn the wheels.

00:05:31.319 --> 00:05:33.111
Sense, decide, connect, act.

00:05:33.111 --> 00:05:36.631
You can build exactly this kind of machine in RoverLab.

00:05:37.911 --> 00:05:40.332
Part seven. Hardware limitations.

00:05:40.932 --> 00:05:44.206
So why can't AI simply do everything? Very often,

00:05:44.206 --> 00:05:45.742
the answer is hardware.

00:05:46.022 --> 00:05:48.971
This links to Chapter six of our study guide:

00:05:48.971 --> 00:05:50.674
Strengths and Limitations.

00:05:50.954 --> 00:05:55.086
Processing capacity is finite. So is memory capacity.

00:05:55.366 --> 00:05:57.565
Every calculation consumes energy,

00:05:57.565 --> 00:06:00.994
and that energy turns into heat that must be removed.

00:06:01.274 --> 00:06:04.456
Network bandwidth limits how fast data can travel.

00:06:04.456 --> 00:06:06.811
And all of this hardware costs money.

00:06:07.091 --> 00:06:10.112
So engineers are building new kinds of hardware.

00:06:10.392 --> 00:06:15.612
Dedicated AI accelerators. Neural processing units, or NPUs.

00:06:15.892 --> 00:06:20.794
More energy-efficient processors, built with advanced semiconductor manufacturing.

00:06:21.074 --> 00:06:22.714
And neuromorphic computing:

00:06:22.714 --> 00:06:25.265
chips inspired by the way the brain works.

00:06:25.545 --> 00:06:29.180
The future of AI depends not only on better algorithms,

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.947
but on better, faster and more energy-efficient hardware.

00:06:33.227 --> 00:06:36.135
So the next time an answer appears on your screen,

00:06:36.135 --> 00:06:37.938
remember the machine behind it.
